 \section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}
 
 Aggregate biocapacity cannot reveal how its underlying land classes are changing. In the two-land model, conversion raises total $B$ at the margin when fast-land contribution per hectare exceeds capital land's effective value ($b_f>\Ups(q)$), while $A_c$ falls if restoration is off (Proposition~\ref{prop:mask}). Other model terms determine whether total $B$ rises overall. This is a conditional accounting mechanism, not an empirical census of global physical stocks.
 
-The reduced fixed-demand comparison follows a single balance branch to the specified minimum-area boundary without a fold, hysteresis, or critical slowing. That result is limited to the stated comparison and does not characterize every trajectory of the delayed model. Realized paths remain history-dependent: when shortfalls subside and restoration is off, $A_c$ remains below its pre-overshoot level, but it does not continue to decline at the balance point. In the representative baseline run, total model biocapacity rises $39\%$ while ecological-capital area falls $46.16\%$, its per-hectare quality term $\Ups(q)$ falls $10.33\%$, and its modeled contribution $\Ups(q)A_c$ falls $51.72\%$.
